    "done_when": "The manifest names the caller that put the harness home in the worktree, as file:line, with how the wrong directory reached it.\n\nA new test, tests/test_harness_home_is_not_in_the_worktree.py, composes a dispatch's harness environment for both dialects. It uses a temporary config home, a synthesised worktree, and a run directory outside it. It asserts that CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR and CODEX_HOME resolve under the run directory and not under the worktree. It fails at the base revision (logged) and passes at the head. The negative control points the harness home back at the worktree in a scratch copy, and the test fails, with the mutation on the red log's first line and an EXIT= line after pytest's output.\n\nThe gate is the new test plus the existing test files that exercise harness_home. It exits 0, or shows no added failing ids against the base, with both arms recorded as log paths under baseline_suite and after_suite. The test neither reads nor writes the real ~/.config/reckon.",
    "estimated_hours": 2.0,
    "goal": "Plan crew-observability-truth, followup f-cot-worker-scratch-dirties-every-worktree. reckon/_backends.py:1911-1912 declares a per-run harness home (harness/ for the claude dialect, codex-home/ for codex). harness_home() documents that it sits in the run directory, the parent of the node's manifest. Yet finished worktrees under ~/Code/.reckon-worktrees/.../s22-coord/ contain untracked harness/ and codex-home/ at their root. Seen on name-the-ended-turn-in-its-reason and reflex-keys-on-reviewed-run-and-head, whose manifests live in their run directories under ~/.config/reckon/crew/runs/. So gc classifies every merged worktree as dirty.\n\nFind which caller resolves the harness home inside the worktree, and make the harness home land in the run directory. If the wrong argument is composed in reckon/crew/dispatch.py, which another coordinator's node currently holds, stop and report it as a blocker naming the line.",
